          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My father had a heart attack at 55. What does that mean for my own risk, and how does guidance use family history when deciding about a statin?",
          "answer": "If you ever have chest pain, pressure, or breathlessness, especially with sweating, nausea, or pain spreading to your arm, neck or jaw, call emergency services right away. Do not wait to see whether it passes or drive yourself.\n\nYour father's heart attack at 55 is a family history factor. It does not by itself tell you your own risk. The guidance we could access says your risk depends mainly on your own age, sex, cholesterol, blood pressure, diabetes and smoking status (*Coronary artery disease*). It does not give the age cutoff that defines \"premature\" or \"early onset\" for a father's heart disease, so we cannot confirm whether his age counts under that guidance. A clinician can confirm this.\n\nFor statin decisions, the lipid guidance (*Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation*) starts with an estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk, calculated from your own measurements. Family history of premature cardiovascular disease is a \"risk-enhancing feature.\" It does not decide the question alone. It can shift the decision when your estimated risk is borderline (5% to below 7.5%) or intermediate (7.5% to below 20%), and for borderline risk the guidance favors therapy where such features are present. The guidance we could retrieve does not say how strong the evidence is that family history adds to risk. We found no disagreement between authorities on family history and statins, though the sources did disagree on other topics, such as LDL targets.\n\nOther factors the coronary guidance lists include smoking, high blood pressure, raised LDL cholesterol, diabetes, obesity, physical inactivity, a diet high in saturated fat and low in vegetables and fibre, and chronic kidney disease. Many of these can be changed. We could not retrieve detailed diet or activity guidance.\n\nTo take to a clinician:\n- Does my father's age at his heart attack count as \"premature\" under the guidance you use?\n- What is my estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk, using my age, cholesterol, blood pressure, smoking and diabetes status?\n- Does my family history change that estimate, and would a coronary artery calcium scan help if my risk is borderline or intermediate?\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
